\section*{Instructions for reproducing the analyses}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Instructions for reproducing the analyses}

In acknowledgement of recent calls for more transparent and fully reproducible research practices across virtually all sub-disciplines of psychology \citep{Klein:2018practical, Tackett:2019introduction, Mellor:2021improving, Grahe:2018another, Hagger:2019embracing, Gilmore:2020advancing, Condon:2017jz}, the supplemental materials for this project are extensive. To reproduce this file, download the zip folder under `OSF Storage' from the OSF project page (\href{tinyurl.com/2a8vx6n8}{tinyurl.com/2a8vx6n8}). Open it on your computer, making note of the location where it is stored (and relocating, if you choose). Once the data have been downloaded, it should be possible to reproduce the published results by using the \texttt{R} code in the ``.Rnw'' version of this document (this includes several analyses --- code chunks --- which have been suppressed in the compiled PDF version for the sake of readability).

The code can either be executed manually in \texttt{R} or by compiling the file with \href{http://yihui.name/knitr/demo/manual/}{Knitr} \citep{Xie:2015vc} or \href{http://www.stat.uni-muenchen.de/~leisch/Sweave/Sweave-manual.pdf}{Sweave} \citep{Sweave} (some editing of the text passages may be required once the file is opened in RStudio or text editing software). The current PDF version was compiled using Knitr in \href{https://www.rstudio.com/}{RStudio} \citep{Gandrud:2015ux} on \today{}.  Those who do not want to use RStudio can open the .Rnw file with a text editor and run the code chunks that begin with ``$<<$'' and end with ``@''. The code mainly uses functions in base \texttt{R} \Sexpr{R.version$major}.\Sexpr{R.version$minor} \citep{R}, \Sexpr{packageDescription("psych")$Package} version \Sexpr{packageDescription("psych")$Version} \citep{Rpsych}, and a few other packages; the names and version numbers are listed in Table \ref{tab:Rpacks}.

