\clearpage

\subsection{Descriptive statistics for the 67 new CRA items}

Next, we begin examining the properties of the CRA items. It is important to note that these items were administered infrequently due to the planned missingness design of the data collection, so we need to better specify the descriptive statistics of the CRA items by themselves. First, we subset the data, describe it, and then look at the number of pairwise administrations. This latter characteristic is important for subsequent evaluation of the structure of the set. We also get the tetrachoric correlation matrix at this stage; note that smoothing was necessary as the matrix was not positive definite.

\begin{scriptsize}
<<Study1.4, eval=TRUE, echo=TRUE, results='markup', fig.show='hide', tidy=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>= 
load(paste(wdpath, "data/study1/CRAdataStudy1version1.rdata", sep = ""))

CRAitemData <- CRAitemData[CRAitemData$servertime < as.POSIXct("2022-06-26 12:00:00", tz="UTC"),]
dim(CRAitemData)

CRAitemSet67 <- subset(CRAitemData, select = c(q_14003:q_14197))
dim(CRAitemSet67) # only the 67 item subset being validated

descCRAitems <- psych::describe(CRAitemSet67)
rm(CRAdata)
gcQuiet()

administrations <- describe(descCRAitems$n)

pwiseAdminsCRAitems <- pairwiseCount(CRAitemSet67, diagonal = TRUE)
pwiseAdminsCRAitemsVec <- as.vector(pwiseAdminsCRAitems[lower.tri(pwiseAdminsCRAitems)])
pwiseAdminsCRAitemsDescribe <- psych::describe(pwiseAdminsCRAitemsVec)
n_obs <- pwiseAdminsCRAitemsDescribe$mean

tetchor_matrix67 <- tetrachoric(CRAitemSet67, smooth = TRUE)
cor_matrix67 <- tetchor_matrix67$rho

@
\end{scriptsize}

The 67 items in this set have been administered an average of \Sexpr{prettyNum(round(administrations$mean, 0), big.mark=",", scientific=F)} times each (\textit{sd} = \Sexpr{prettyNum(round(administrations$sd, 1), big.mark=",", scientific=F)}; \textit{Mdn} = \Sexpr{prettyNum(administrations$median, big.mark=",", scientific=F)}), with a minimum of \Sexpr{prettyNum(administrations$min, big.mark=",", scientific=F)}. However, these values are vastly larger than the pairwise administrations among all \Sexpr{prettyNum(((67*67)-67)/2, big.mark=",", scientific=F)} possible pairs, as these had a mean of only \Sexpr{prettyNum(round(pwiseAdminsCRAitemsDescribe$mean, 1), big.mark=",", scientific=F)} (\textit{sd} = \Sexpr{prettyNum(round(pwiseAdminsCRAitemsDescribe$sd, 1), big.mark=",", scientific=F)}; \textit{Mdn} = \Sexpr{prettyNum(pwiseAdminsCRAitemsDescribe$median, big.mark=",", scientific=F)}). This circumstance --- much higher administration counts per item than pairwise administrations --- is due to the relatively small number of items administered to each participant (often only two). The data collection protocol on the \textbf{[blinded for review]} only dedicates about 10\% of survey content to cognitive abilities assessment, and much of this is taken up by the administration of previously validated scales, i.e., the four original item types described in \citet{Condon:14}. Importantly, the evaluations of validity reported in later sections of this work (e.g., discriminant and convergent validity) benefit from the statistical power of the larger number of individual administrations against other criteria. Evaluations of the correlational structure among items do not, and this prompts consideration of whether the current number of observations is adequate for the structural evaluations conducted here.

Various ``rules of thumb'' have been proposed regarding statistical power for exploratory factor analysis; see \citet{Kyriazos:2018} for an excellent summary. In addition to the typical considerations affecting power (e.g., thresholds for alpha and beta, anticipated effect sizes, and number of observations), issues affecting power in EFA include the number of measured variables, the number of dimensions being extracted (i.e., the factors or components), and the observed orientation of the variables to those dimensions (i.e., the communalities and the loadings). This complexity contributes to the appeal of using generic rules to estimate power for EFA, but it should be noted that these rules are often quite arbitrary. The categories provided by Comrey and colleagues \citep{Comrey:73}, for example, are overly simplistic --- they suggest grading sample sizes independent of contextual factors: very poor (N=50), poor (N=100), fair (N=200), good (N=300), very good (N=500), and excellent (N=1,000).

More nuanced rules are often based on detailed simulations \citep{Dimitrov:2014, Guadagnoli:1988}. The most applicable for EFA specifically are framed around the ratio of observations (N) to variables \citep[p;][]{Nunnally:1994, Dimitrov:2014}. Recommendations range from N/p = 1 for larger samples to 5 \citep{Gorsuch:83} or even 10 \citep{Nunnally:1994, Wang:2019} when samples sizes are small (i.e., Ns of roughly 100 might only be considered marginally adequate for small numbers of variables). A particularly useful recommendation from \citet{Tinsley:1987} proposes 5 to 10 observations per variable up to N=300, beyond which this proportion can be relaxed \citep{DeVellis:2021, Kyriazos:2018}. The data used here comfortably exceeds these recommendations --- again, the mean and median are \Sexpr{prettyNum(round(pwiseAdminsCRAitemsDescribe$mean, 1), big.mark=",", scientific=F)} and \Sexpr{prettyNum(round(pwiseAdminsCRAitemsDescribe$median, 2), big.mark=",", scientific=F)}, respectively, and the minimum is \Sexpr{prettyNum(round(pwiseAdminsCRAitemsDescribe$min, 2), big.mark=",", scientific=F)}.

\subsection{Dimensionality of the 67 new CRA items}

The next step is to evaluate the unidimensionality of the items, as this is a key assumption to fulfill when using item response theory-based procedures (and we want to use these in subsequent analyses, if possible). We begin by calling \texttt{vss()} to generate several test statistics for various factor solutions.

\begin{scriptsize}
<<Study1.5, eval=TRUE, echo=TRUE, results='markup', fig.show='hide', tidy=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>= 
fitstats <- psych::vss(cor_matrix67, n = 4, n.obs = n_obs, rotate = "oblimin", diagonal = FALSE, fm = "mle", plot = FALSE)

fit_table <- fitstats$vss.stats

fit_table <- fit_table %>% 
  dplyr::mutate(Factors = seq(1:4)) %>% # Look at fit indices for 1-4 factor solutions
  relocate(Factors)

fit_table <- fit_table %>%
  dplyr::select(Factors, chisq, fit, RMSEA, BIC, SRMR) # Selecting preregistered fit indices

fit_table <- fit_table %>%
  mutate(Map = fitstats$map) %>%
  dplyr::rename(VSS = fit,
         Chisq = chisq)

# suppressMessages(suppressWarnings(fa.parallel(cor_matrix67)))
# Parallel analysis suggests that the number of factors =  2
# and the number of components =  1 
@
\end{scriptsize}

\begin{scriptsize}
<<Study1.6, eval=TRUE, echo=FALSE, results='asis', fig.show='hide', tidy=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>= 

comment <- list(pos = list(0), command = NULL)
comment$pos[[1]] <- c(nrow(fit_table))
comment$command <- c(paste("\\hline\n",
                          "\\multicolumn{7}{l}{Note: VSS shown here reflects complexity of 1. SRMR is the standardized root mean square residual.}\\\\\n", "\\multicolumn{7}{l}{MAP is Velicer's Minimum Average Partial criterion.}\\\\\n", sep = ""))

options(xtable.NA.string = "NA")
fit_table1 <- xtable(fit_table[,1:ncol(fit_table)], digits=0, caption='Fit indices for the 1 to 4 factor solutions with 67 items', label='tab:fit', align= c("R{1.5cm}", "R{1.5cm}", "R{1.5cm}", "R{1.5cm}", "R{1.5cm}", "R{1.5cm}", "R{1.5cm}", "R{1.5cm}"))
names(fit_table1) <- c("Factors", "Chi Square", "VSS", "RMSEA", "BIC", "SRMR", "MAP")
digits(fit_table1) <- c(0,0,0,2,2,0,3,3)

print(fit_table1, add.to.row = comment, hline.after = c(-1,0,NULL), size='scriptsize', include.rownames=FALSE, floating = TRUE)
@
\end{scriptsize}

We evaluate the fit for solutions with up to 4 factors, and then create Table \ref{tab:fit} with the output. Table \ref{tab:fit} displays model fit indices for solutions with one through four factors. The optimal number of factors is inconsistent across fit indices. Support for the single-factor model is indicated by the VSS (Very Simple Structure) criterion, which suggests that extracting one factor yields the most interpretable results. The SRMR suggests at least adequate fit (i.e., less than .08) for all solutions. The chi-square value, BIC (Bayesian Information Criterion), and MAP (Velicer’s Minimal Average Partial) criterion were minimized in models with a higher number of factors. RMSEA values were high for all models, far exceeding the standard threshold for adequate fit (i.e., of less than .08). Reasons for notably poor fit across all solutions as indicated by the RMSEA are unclear, but recent evidence suggests that the RMSEA --— in addition to other popular fit indices --— is ill-suited for use in exploratory factor analysis because it almost always leads to over-factoring \citep{Montoya:2021}. Consequently, RMSEA values are reported for the purpose of adhering to the preregistered plan of analysis but are not used to guide interpretation regarding the factor structure of the CRA items. Figure \ref{fig:faparallel} shows the results of parallel analysis suggesting two factors or one principal component.

\begin{figure}
\ffigbox{\includegraphics[width=.45\textwidth]{figure/faparallel.png}}{\caption{Parallel analysis based on the 67 CRA items using principal components (PC) and factor analysis (FA)}\label{fig:faparallel}}
\end{figure}

The \texttt{omega()} function in the psych package is used to show coefficient $\omega_{h}$ and (for internal consistency) $\alpha$ and $\omega_{t}$.

\begin{scriptsize}
<<Study1.7, eval=TRUE, echo=TRUE, results='markup', fig.show='hide', tidy=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>= 
omega_results <- psych::omega(cor_matrix67, plot = FALSE)
omega.h <- round(omega_results$omega_h,2)
omega.alpha <- round(omega_results$alpha,2)
omega.t <- round(omega_results$omega.tot,2)
@
\end{scriptsize}

The items have high internal consistency in this data set ($\alpha$ = \Sexpr{omega.alpha}; $\omega_{t}$ = \Sexpr{omega.t}). Similarly, the omega general value ($\omega_{h}$ = \Sexpr{omega.h}) suggests strong unidimensionality in the CRA items. To help visualize this, the next code chunk plots the correlations among items after they have been sorted by factor loading, with a one factor model specified (sorting by factor loading is only done to organize the presentation of items). We also extract the empirically-observed confidence intervals around the tetrachoric correlations here. 

\begin{scriptsize}
<<Study1.8, eval=TRUE, echo=FALSE, results='hide', fig.show='hide', tidy=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>= 
CRA_fa <- suppressMessages(suppressWarnings(fa(cor_matrix67, nfactors = 1)))
CRA_fa_sort <- fa.sort(CRA_fa)
rows_CRA <- rownames(CRA_fa_sort$loadings)

cor_matrix67_plot <- cor_matrix67[rows_CRA, rows_CRA]

min_cor_value <- min(cor_matrix67)
avg_cor_value <- mean(cor_matrix67)

CIs <- corCi(cor_matrix67, n.iter=1000, n=pwiseAdminsCRAitemsDescribe$mean)
CIs2 <- as.data.frame(CIs$r.ci)
CIs2$range <- CIs2$upper-CIs2$lower
CIs2$sig <- ifelse(CIs2$range > CIs2$upper, 0, 1)
CIs2$sigNeg <- ifelse(CIs2$upper < 0, 1, 0)

maxCI <- max(CIs2$range)
minCI <- min(CIs2$range)
meanCI <- mean(CIs2$range)
overlappingZero <- nrow(CIs2)-sum(CIs2$sig)
sigNeg <- sum(CIs2$sigNeg)
@
\end{scriptsize}

\begin{scriptsize}
<<Study1.9, eval=TRUE, echo=FALSE, results='hide', tidy=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>=
file_path = paste(wdpath, "figure/singlefactorcors67.png", sep = "")
png(height=1800, width=1800, file=file_path, type = "cairo")
corrplot(cor_matrix67_plot, method = "color", tl.col = "black",
         bg = "White", tl.srt = 0, type = "full", title = "",
         col = COL2('RdBu',200), tl.pos = 'n')
dev.off()

file_path= paste(wdpath, "figure/singlefactorcors67.eps", sep="")
setEPS()
postscript(height=200, width=200, file=file_path)
corrplot(cor_matrix67_plot, method = "color", tl.col = "black",
         bg = "White", tl.srt = 0, type = "full", title = "",
         col = COL2('RdBu',200), tl.pos = 'n')
dev.off()

@
\end{scriptsize}

The correlations are shown in Figure \ref{fig:singlefactorcors67}. The plot provides clear evidence of a strong general factor (average \textit{r} = \Sexpr{round(avg_cor_value,3)}), though there are a small number of correlations between items with point estimates below 0 --- as low as \Sexpr{round(min_cor_value,3)} --- indicated with pale red tones in the lower right corner of the figure. The range of the 95\% confidence intervals around these correlations (i.e., the range from the high to low end of the confidence interval) had a mean of \Sexpr{round(meanCI,3)} (minimum $=$ \Sexpr{round(minCI,3)} and maximum = \Sexpr{round(maxCI,3)}). Of the 2,211 pairwise correlations, \Sexpr{overlappingZero} had confidence intervals overlapping with zero; \Sexpr{sigNeg} of the remaining \Sexpr{prettyNum(2211-overlappingZero, big.mark=",", scientific=F)} had point estimates below 0. The correlations among these ``significantly'' negative associations are \Sexpr{round(cor_matrix67["q_14058","q_14082"],2)} (q\textunderscore 14058 and q\textunderscore 14082) and \Sexpr{round(cor_matrix67["q_14096","q_14148"],2)} (q\textunderscore 14096 and q\textunderscore 14148). The content of these items can be seen in Table \ref{tab:parameters}. It is unclear why these are negatively associated. One possibility is that there is a cohort effect that makes knowledge/familiarity of the words in each pair relatively incompatible. A second possibility is that one (or both) of the items in each pair prompts a near alternative among the compound words in the matching item. Further research into this topic is warranted, but none of these items are dropped as they demonstrate adequate psychometric properties in other respects.

\begin{figure}
\ffigbox{\includegraphics[width=.75\textwidth]{figure/singlefactorcors67.png}}{\caption{Correlations among all 67 CRA items sorted by general factor loadings.}\label{fig:singlefactorcors67}}
\end{figure}

\clearpage

\subsection{IRT-based analyses of the 67 new CRA items}

Supported by the evidence of unidimensionality, the item pool was evaluated using item response theory procedures. In the chunk below, we extract the IRT parameters, plot the information curves for the items and the set, and evaluate the range of item difficulties.

\begin{scriptsize}
<<Study1.10, eval=TRUE, echo=FALSE, results='hide', fig.show='hide', tidy=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>= 
# get the IRT model of the response data
IRT_model <- suppressWarnings(mirt(CRAitemSet67, 1, itemtype = "2PL", constrain = NULL))
summary(IRT_model)

# table the item parameters
# note that you must specify IRTpars=TRUE to get difficulties instead of easiness
IRT_model_parameters <- coef(IRT_model, IRTpars=TRUE, simplify = TRUE)
CRAparameters <- IRT_model_parameters$items[,1:2]
CRAparameters <- data.frame(CRAparameters, descCRAitems$mean, descCRAitems$se, descCRAitems$n)
colnames(CRAparameters) <- c("disc", "diff", "mean", "se", "n")

CRAparametersSortedByDisc <- CRAparameters[order(CRAparameters[,"disc"]),]
CRAparametersSortedByDiff <- CRAparameters[order(CRAparameters[,"diff"]),]
CRAparametersSortedByMean <- CRAparameters[order(CRAparameters[,"mean"]),]
@
\end{scriptsize}

\begin{scriptsize}
<<Study1.11, eval=TRUE, echo=FALSE, results='hide', tidy=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>=
# plot the item characteristic curves for all items separately and together

file_path = paste(wdpath, "figure/ICCs67.png", sep = "")
png(height=600, width=600, file=file_path, type = "cairo")
plot(IRT_model, type = 'trace', facet_items = TRUE)
dev.off()

file_path= paste(wdpath, "figure/ICCs67.eps", sep="")
setEPS()
postscript(height=100, width=100, file=file_path)
plot(IRT_model, type = 'trace', facet_items = TRUE)
dev.off()

file_path = paste(wdpath, "figure/ICCscombined67.png", sep = "")
png(height=600, width=600, file=file_path, type = "cairo")
plot(IRT_model, type = 'trace', facet_items = FALSE, auto.key = FALSE)
dev.off()

file_path= paste(wdpath, "figure/ICCscombined67.eps", sep="")
setEPS()
postscript(height=100, width=100, file=file_path)
plot(IRT_model, type = 'trace', facet_items = TRUE)
dev.off()

panel1 <- findcolnumber(CRAitemSet67, "q_14043")
panel2 <- findcolnumber(CRAitemSet67, "q_14072")
panel3 <- findcolnumber(CRAitemSet67, "q_14048")
panel4 <- findcolnumber(CRAitemSet67, "q_14031")

file_path = paste(wdpath, "figure/discriminations67.png", sep = "")
png(height=600, width=600, file=file_path, type = "cairo")
plot(IRT_model, type = 'trace', which.items = c(panel3, panel4, panel1, panel2), facet_items = TRUE)
dev.off()

file_path= paste(wdpath, "figure/discriminations67.eps", sep="")
setEPS()
postscript(height=100, width=100, file=file_path)
plot(IRT_model, type = 'trace', which.items = c(panel3, panel4, panel1, panel2), facet_items = TRUE)
dev.off()

@
\end{scriptsize}

Figure \ref{fig:ICCscombined} shows the probability functions --- that is, the probability of correct/incorrect responses across the range of difficulties --- for all the items together in one plot. Figure \ref{fig:ICCs} shows the probability functions for all 67 items separately and labeled. Together, these figures are very useful for evaluating the properties of the CRA items individually and as a set.

\begin{figure}
\ffigbox{\includegraphics[width=.75\textwidth]{figure/ICCscombined67.png}}{\caption{Item probability functions for all 67 CRA items together.}\label{fig:ICCscombined}}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}
\ffigbox{\includegraphics[width=.95\textwidth]{figure/ICCs67.png}}{\caption{Item probability functions for all 67 CRA items.}\label{fig:ICCs}}
\end{figure}

Figure \ref{fig:ICCscombined} shows that most items have difficulties greater than zero (\textit{m} = \Sexpr{round(mean(CRAparametersSortedByDisc[,"diff"]),2)}; \textit{Mdn} = \Sexpr{round(median(CRAparametersSortedByDisc[,"diff"]),2)}). As mentioned above, difficulty values (in this model) reflect the location of the intercept where the probabilities of correct and incorrect responses are equal. Only \Sexpr{length(CRAparametersSortedByDiff$diff[CRAparametersSortedByDiff$diff<0])} of the 67 items have difficulty coefficients below zero, and this indicates that most items are generally quite hard. Note that the mean proportion of correct responses for each item is highly (negatively) correlated with item difficulty (\textit{r} = \Sexpr{round(cor(CRAparametersSortedByDiff$diff, CRAparametersSortedByDiff$mean),2)}), as expected. Given the content of these items, moderately high difficulties are desirable, but items with excessively high difficulty would not be particularly useful as there will be little variance across respondents (nearly all respondents fail to identify the correct answer). There are two items with difficulties more than 4 standard deviations above the mean ability level: item q\textunderscore 14043 (difficulty = \Sexpr{round(CRAparametersSortedByDisc["q_14043","diff"],2)}) and item q\textunderscore 14175 (difficulty = \Sexpr{round(CRAparametersSortedByDisc["q_14175","diff"],2)}).

Figures \ref{fig:ICCscombined} and \ref{fig:ICCs} also demonstrate that most of the items are adequately discriminant (\textit{m} = \Sexpr{round(mean(CRAparametersSortedByDisc[,"disc"]),2)}; \textit{Mdn} = \Sexpr{round(median(CRAparametersSortedByDisc[,"disc"]),2)}), though there are a few exceptions. This implies that the items are able to discriminate well between individuals with relatively similar levels of associative ability around the difficulty intercept, as a small increase in associative ability in this range substantially increases the probability of correct responding. This is depicted visually by the slope of the item probability functions. Of the 67 items, two have discrimination parameters near .5, a value that is commonly deemed a lower bound of acceptability \citep{An:2014, Debeer:2013}. Note that it may also be reasonable to drop a few other items with relatively low discriminations, but there is a case to be made for keeping items that cover a wide range of discriminations, especially if they are all above an acceptable threshold \citep{Cheng:2015}. This argument mainly rests on the increased flexibility provided by a diverse item set for computer adaptive testing applications.

For reference, Figure \ref{fig:discriminations} highlights the discrimination for several items as examples. Item q\textunderscore 14043 has among the lowest discriminations of all 67 items at \Sexpr{round(CRAparametersSortedByDisc["q_14043","disc"],2)}. In the top right, item q\textunderscore 14072 has a low but adequate discrimination of \Sexpr{round(CRAparametersSortedByDisc["q_14072","disc"],2)}. Items q\textunderscore 14154 and q\textunderscore 14031 have the median and maximum discrimination values, respectively (\Sexpr{round(CRAparametersSortedByDisc["q_14048","disc"],2)} and \Sexpr{round(CRAparametersSortedByDisc["q_14031","disc"],2)}).

\begin{figure}
\ffigbox{\includegraphics[width=.75\textwidth]{figure/discriminations67.png}}{\caption{Examples of varying discrimination values among the CRA items.}\label{fig:discriminations}}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Identifying and reporting on the final set of (65) new CRA items}

As suggested in the prior section, there are two items close to the lower threshold of acceptable discrimination values (slightly above and/or at 0.5). These items are q\textunderscore 14043 and q\textunderscore 14023. Several other items have discriminations between 0.55 and 0.65 --- these are items q\textunderscore 14011, q\textunderscore 14003, q\textunderscore 14175, and q\textunderscore 14058. Note that 3 of the latter set of items have a correct answer beginning with a common letter (``s''), and it may be that this reflects a somewhat effective guessing strategy to the detriment of the discrimination of those items. All of these six items are listed below:

\begin{itemize}
\item q\textunderscore 14043 --- \textbf{prompt:} front  side  bed; \textbf{response options:} o,p,w,a,t,l; \textbf{answer:} l (lake)
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
\item q\textunderscore 14023 --- \textbf{prompt:} card  board  keeper; \textbf{response options:} d,t,a,o,s,r; \textbf{answer:} s (score)
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
\item q\textunderscore 14011 --- \textbf{prompt:} line  rocket  box; \textbf{response options:} s,m,p,d,v,o; \textbf{answer:} s (sky)
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
\item q\textunderscore 14003 --- \textbf{prompt:} bed  sheet  wide; \textbf{response options:} m,y,s,v,k,a; \textbf{answer:} s (spread)
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
\item q\textunderscore 14175 --- \textbf{prompt:} line  water  war; \textbf{response options:} p,f,s,m,n,k; \textbf{answer:} f (front)
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
\item q\textunderscore 14058 --- \textbf{prompt:} bed  jet  blood; \textbf{response options:} f,m,b,s,r,w; \textbf{answer:} s (stream)
\end{itemize}

Ultimately, it was decided to drop the least discriminating of these items (q\textunderscore 14043 and q\textunderscore 14023) and retain the rest. The decision to retain the latter 4 (q\textunderscore 14011, q\textunderscore 14003, q\textunderscore 14175, and q\textunderscore 14058) was based on the case for retaining as many items as possible for the sake of potentially developing a CAT at some future point. However, the decision to retain these items should be reevaluate in subsequent research, especially if contradicting evidence is found in other samples (that is, if the items continue to have borderline discrimination values).

In the remainder of this section, we re-run the code from the prior subsections after removing the 2 undesirable items; this leaves only 65 items in total. The new analyses generate updated versions of the correlation plot and item probability functions; see Figures \ref{fig:singlefactorcorsFinal}, \ref{fig:ICCscombinedFinal}, and \ref{fig:ICCsFinal}.

\begin{scriptsize}
<<Study1.12, eval=TRUE, echo=FALSE, results='hide', tidy=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>=
itemsToDropDisc <- rownames(subset(CRAparametersSortedByDisc, disc < .52))
itemsToDrop67 <- itemsToDropDisc
length(itemsToDrop67)
# As of 8/19/2022, two items are dropped: 14043 and 14023

if (length(itemsToDrop67)>0) {
CRAitemSetFinal <- CRAitemSet67[, -which(names(CRAitemSet67) %in% itemsToDrop67)]
} else {
CRAitemSetFinal <- CRAitemSet67
}

dim(CRAitemSetFinal)

CRAitemNumbersStudy1 <- colnames(CRAitemSetFinal)
descCRAitems <- psych::describe(CRAitemSetFinal)

tetchor_matrix <- tetrachoric(CRAitemSetFinal, smooth = TRUE)
cor_matrix <- tetchor_matrix$rho

omega_results <- suppressWarnings(psych::omega(cor_matrix, plot = FALSE))
omega.h <- round(omega_results$omega_h,2)
omega.alpha <- round(omega_results$alpha,2)
omega.t <- round(omega_results$omega.tot,2)

CRA_fa <- suppressMessages(suppressWarnings(fa(cor_matrix, nfactors = 1)))
CRA_fa_sort <- fa.sort(CRA_fa)
rows_CRA <- rownames(CRA_fa_sort$loadings)

cor_matrix_plot <- cor_matrix[rows_CRA, rows_CRA]

min_cor_value <- min(cor_matrix)
avg_cor_value <- mean(cor_matrix)

CIs <- corCi(cor_matrix, n.iter=1000, n=pwiseAdminsCRAitemsDescribe$mean)
CIs2 <- as.data.frame(CIs$r.ci)
CIs2$range <- CIs2$upper-CIs2$lower
CIs2$sig <- ifelse(CIs2$range > CIs2$upper, 0, 1)
CIs2$sigNeg <- ifelse(CIs2$upper < 0, 1, 0)

maxCI <- max(CIs2$range)
minCI <- min(CIs2$range)
meanCI <- mean(CIs2$range)
overlappingZero <- nrow(CIs2)-sum(CIs2$sig)
sigNeg <- sum(CIs2$sigNeg)

# get the IRT model of the response data
IRT_model <- suppressWarnings(mirt(CRAitemSetFinal, 1, itemtype = "2PL", constrain = NULL, technical = list(NCYCLES=2000)))
summary(IRT_model)
@
\end{scriptsize}


\begin{scriptsize}
<<Study1.13, eval=TRUE, echo=FALSE, results='hide', tidy=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>=
# plot the cor matrix and item characteristic curves for all items separately and together
# comment out the remainder of this chunk when ready to publish

file_path = paste(wdpath, "figure/singlefactorcorsFinal.png", sep = "")
png(height=1800, width=1800, file=file_path, type = "cairo")
corrplot(cor_matrix_plot, method = "color", tl.col = "black",
         bg = "White", tl.srt = 0, type = "full", title = "",
         col = COL2('RdBu',200), tl.pos = 'n')
dev.off()

file_path= paste(wdpath, "figure/singlefactorcorsFinal.eps", sep="")
setEPS()
postscript(height=200, width=200, file=file_path)
corrplot(cor_matrix_plot, method = "color", tl.col = "black",
         bg = "White", tl.srt = 0, type = "full", title = "",
         col = COL2('RdBu',200), tl.pos = 'n')
dev.off()

file_path = paste(wdpath, "figure/ICCsFinal.png", sep = "")
png(height=600, width=600, file=file_path, type = "cairo")
plot(IRT_model, type = 'trace', facet_items = TRUE)
dev.off()

file_path= paste(wdpath, "figure/ICCsFinal.eps", sep="")
setEPS()
