In Table \ref{tab:parameters}, we present the results numerically, overlapping with the figures to some extent. This includes coefficients for the item difficulties (the location of the 50/50 probability of a correct response) and discrimination parameters (the slopes), as well as the mean proportion of correct responses and the standard error of the means.

Figure \ref{fig:irttestfunction} shows the joint information for the final set of CRA items across the range of difficulty along with the standard error. As the standard error is akin to the inverse of reliability (more specifically, reliability is 1 minus the square of the standard error over the standard deviation), Figure \ref{fig:irttestfunction} indicates that the test is particularly reliable from the range of difficulty of about -0.5 sd to +3 sd. The marginal reliability across all respondents in these data is high at \Sexpr{round(marginalReliability,2)} \citep{Thissen:2001}.

\begin{figure}
\ffigbox{\includegraphics[width=.75\textwidth]{figure/irttestfunction.png}}{\caption{Test information (blue) and standard error (red) functions for the new CRA items.}\label{fig:irttestfunction}}
\end{figure}

The final step of the IRT analyses for the new items is to generate latent trait scores for respondents based on the item parameters from this 2PL model. The next code chunk does just that, appending the scale scores to the end of our data frame based on responses to the final set of new items.

\begin{scriptsize}
<<Study1.17, eval=TRUE, echo=TRUE, results='asis', tidy=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>=
# use the model to generate IRT scores
CRAitemSetFinal <- CRAitemSetFinal[,1:65]

empty_rows = which(is.nan(rowMeans(CRAitemSetFinal, na.rm = TRUE)))
CRAitemSetTemp <- CRAitemSetFinal[-empty_rows,]
scored <- fscores(object = IRT_model, response.pattern = CRAitemSetTemp)
CRAitemSetFinal[-empty_rows,c("CRA_new_IRT_score", "CRA_new_IRT_score_SE")] <- scored
save(CRAitemSetFinal, file = paste(wdpath, "data/study1/CRAitemSetFinal.rdata", sep = ""))
CRAnewItemNames <- colnames(CRAitemSetFinal)
IRT_model_ICAR_CRAnew <- IRT_model
save(IRT_model_ICAR_CRAnew, CRAnewItemNames, file = paste(wdpath, "data/study1/IRTmodelCRAnewItems.rdata", sep = ""))
@
\end{scriptsize}

\clearpage

\subsection{Dimensionality and IRT analyses of 30 original CRAT items}

\textbf{NOTE:} \textit{The analyses of this sub-section are included here because (a) they are based on the administration of CRAT items (with a mulitple choice response format) to the sample reported on above, and (b) the same IRT procedures used in the previous sub-sections are used again here to identify IRT parameters for these CRAT items for use in Study 2. However, these analyses are not elaborated on extensively in the manuscript as the focus of Study 1 is mainly on the new CRA item set. In Study 4, a more complete pool of CRA items is evaluated together to produce more generalizable IRT parameters, and these are recommended for use by other research teams.}

In addition to the new item set described above (67 original items winnowed down to 65), data were collected on 30 items from an extant CRA item pool \citep[the ``CRAT'' items;][]{Bowden:2003CRAT} using the revised, multiple-choice response format. These items are traditionally administered with an open-ended response format. In this section, we evaluate the dimensionality and IRT-based item properties of those items as an independent set of 30. This is done as part of validating the new set of items against the older set using the same response choice format and scoring method, though it requires additional evaluation of the psychometric properties of the older set (to support the use of IRT scoring).

In the code chunk below, we evaluated the 30 CRAT items independently; the code is not shown in the PDF as it is duplicative of the methods used earlier with the new item set. As with some of the new items, there was one item among these 30 that had a discrimination near the threshold (item q\textunderscore 14317 $=$ .503); it was dropped. In addition, several of the remaining items had very steep discrimination thresholds, suggesting that conditional (correct/incorrect) response patterns existed among them --- this is known as local dependence among the items. To retain as many as possible, we dropped items one-by-one until the model resolved without any items having excessively high discrimination values (this is documented in the .Rnw version of this file). In the end, this left 22 items from the original 30; 1 was dropped due to low discrimination and 7 were dropped to resolve local dependence. The 7 items dropped due to local dependence were:

\begin{itemize}
\item q\textunderscore 14307 --- \textbf{prompt:} way  board  sleep; \textbf{response options:} o,w,h,d,l,s; \textbf{answer:} w (walk)
\item q\textunderscore 14318 --- \textbf{prompt:} land  hand  house; \textbf{response options:} a,g,e,l,w,f; \textbf{answer:} f (farm)
\item q\textunderscore 14310 --- \textbf{prompt:} fence  card  master; \textbf{response options:} p,t,f,v,l,d; \textbf{answer:} p (post)
\item q\textunderscore 14308 --- \textbf{prompt:} tail  water  flood; \textbf{response options:} g,y,r,l,v,s; \textbf{answer:} g (gate)
\item q\textunderscore 14315 --- \textbf{prompt:} hold  print  stool; \textbf{response options:} d,l,f,c,b,w; \textbf{answer:} f (foot)
\item q\textunderscore 14298 --- \textbf{prompt:} fox  man  peep; \textbf{response options:} r,h,k,m,a,t; \textbf{answer:} h (hole)
\item q\textunderscore 14299 --- \textbf{prompt:} shine beam struck; \textbf{response options:} r,m,t,c,a,f; \textbf{answer:} m (moon)
\end{itemize}

Note that this does not mean the items above are necessarily ``bad'' or invalid by themselves (though they may be --- more work would be needed to determine this). But, they were removed here because it would not be appropriate to generate IRT-based scores based, in part, on items that are not independent from the remaining items in the set. This also has relevance for Study 2, as described in that sections.

\begin{scriptsize}
<<Study1.18, eval=TRUE, echo=FALSE, results='hide', tidy=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>=
rm(list=ls()[!(ls() %in% c('findcolnumber', 'itemsToDrop67', 'wdpath', 'CRAitemNumbersStudy1', 'score_ICAR_IRT'))])
load(paste(wdpath, "data/study1/CRAdataStudy1version1.rdata", sep = ""))

CRAdata <- CRAitemData[CRAitemData$servertime < as.POSIXct("2022-06-26 12:00:00", tz="UTC"),]
dim(CRAdata)

allNew <- unique(c(CRAitemNumbersStudy1, itemsToDrop67))
CRAitemSet97 <- subset(CRAdata, select = c(q_14003:q_14318))
CRATitemSet30 <- CRAitemSet97[, -which(names(CRAitemSet97) %in% allNew)]

# delete the line below once you figure out the effect of dropping high disc items
CRATitemSettrimmed <- subset(CRATitemSet30, select = -c(q_14317, q_14307, q_14318, q_14310, q_14308, q_14315, q_14298, q_14299))
dim(CRATitemSettrimmed)

CRATitemNumbers <- colnames(CRATitemSettrimmed)
descCRATitems <- psych::describe(CRATitemSettrimmed)
descCRATitemssorted <- descCRATitems[with(descCRATitems, order(-mean)),]

pwiseAdminsCRATitems <- pairwiseCount(CRATitemSettrimmed, diagonal = TRUE)
pwiseAdminsCRATitems <- as.vector(pwiseAdminsCRATitems[lower.tri(pwiseAdminsCRATitems)])
pwiseAdminsCRATitemsDescribe <- psych::describe(pwiseAdminsCRATitems)
n_obstrimmed <- pwiseAdminsCRATitemsDescribe$mean

tetchor_matrixtrimmed <- tetrachoric(CRATitemSettrimmed, smooth = TRUE)
cor_matrixtrimmed <- tetchor_matrixtrimmed$rho

omega_resultstrimmed <- suppressWarnings(psych::omega(cor_matrixtrimmed, plot = FALSE))
omega.htrimmed <- round(omega_resultstrimmed$omega_h,2)
omega.alphatrimmed <- round(omega_resultstrimmed$alpha,2)
omega.ttrimmed <- round(omega_resultstrimmed$omega.tot,2)

CRA_fatrimmed <- suppressMessages(suppressWarnings(fa(cor_matrixtrimmed, nfactors = 1)))
CRA_fa_sorttrimmed <- fa.sort(CRA_fatrimmed)
rows_CRAtrimmed <- rownames(CRA_fa_sorttrimmed$loadings)

cor_matrixtrimmed_plot <- cor_matrixtrimmed[rows_CRAtrimmed, rows_CRAtrimmed]

avg_cor_valuetrimmed <- mean(cor_matrixtrimmed)

CIstrimmed <- corCi(cor_matrixtrimmed, n.iter=1000, n=pwiseAdminsCRATitemsDescribe$mean)
CIstrimmed <- as.data.frame(CIstrimmed$r.ci)
CIstrimmed$range <- CIstrimmed$upper-CIstrimmed$lower
CIstrimmed$sig <- ifelse(CIstrimmed$range > CIstrimmed$upper, 0, 1)
CIstrimmed$sigNeg <- ifelse(CIstrimmed$upper < 0, 1, 0)

maxCItrimmed <- max(CIstrimmed$range)
minCItrimmed <- min(CIstrimmed$range)
meanCItrimmed <- mean(CIstrimmed$range)
overlappingZerotrimmed <- nrow(CIstrimmed)-sum(CIstrimmed$sig)
sigNegtrimmed <- sum(CIstrimmed$sigNeg)

# get the IRT model of the response data
IRT_modeltrimmed <- suppressWarnings(mirt(CRATitemSettrimmed, 1, itemtype = "2PL", constrain = NULL, technical = list(NCYCLES=2000)))
summary(IRT_modeltrimmed)

# table the item parameters
# note that you must specify IRTpars=TRUE to get difficulties instead of easiness
IRT_model_parameterstrimmed <- coef(IRT_modeltrimmed, IRTpars=TRUE, simplify = TRUE)
CRATparameterstrimmed <- IRT_model_parameterstrimmed$items[,1:2]
CRATparameterstrimmed <- data.frame(CRATparameterstrimmed, descCRATitems$mean, descCRATitems$se, descCRATitems$n)
colnames(CRATparameterstrimmed) <- c("discrimination", "difficulty", "mean", "se", "n")

CRATparametersSortedByDisc <- CRATparameterstrimmed[order(CRATparameterstrimmed[,"discrimination"]),]
CRATparametersSortedByDiff <- CRATparameterstrimmed[order(CRATparameterstrimmed[,"difficulty"]),]
CRATparametersSortedByMean <- CRATparameterstrimmed[order(CRATparameterstrimmed[,"mean"]),]

# plot the cor matrix and item characteristic curves for all items separately and together

file_path = paste(wdpath, "figure/singlefactorcorstrimmed.png", sep = "")
png(height=1800, width=1800, file=file_path, type = "cairo")
corrplot(cor_matrixtrimmed_plot, method = "color", tl.col = "black",
         bg = "White", tl.srt = 0, type = "full", title = "",
         col = COL2('RdBu',200), tl.pos = 'n')
dev.off()

file_path= paste(wdpath, "figure/singlefactorcorstrimmed.eps", sep="")
setEPS()
postscript(height=200, width=200, file=file_path)
corrplot(cor_matrixtrimmed_plot, method = "color", tl.col = "black",
         bg = "White", tl.srt = 0, type = "full", title = "",
         col = COL2('RdBu',200), tl.pos = 'n')
dev.off()

file_path = paste(wdpath, "figure/ICCstrimmed.png", sep = "")
png(height=600, width=600, file=file_path, type = "cairo")
plot(IRT_modeltrimmed, type = 'trace', facet_items = TRUE)
dev.off()

file_path= paste(wdpath, "figure/ICCstrimmed.eps", sep="")
setEPS()
postscript(height=100, width=100, file=file_path)
plot(IRT_modeltrimmed, type = 'trace', facet_items = TRUE)
dev.off()

file_path = paste(wdpath, "figure/ICCscombinedtrimmed.png", sep = "")
png(height=600, width=600, file=file_path, type = "cairo")
plot(IRT_modeltrimmed, type = 'trace', facet_items = FALSE, auto.key = FALSE)
dev.off()

file_path= paste(wdpath, "figure/ICCscombinedtrimmed.eps", sep="")
setEPS()
postscript(height=100, width=100, file=file_path)
plot(IRT_modeltrimmed, type = 'trace', facet_items = TRUE)
dev.off()

# plot the test information function with the standard error

file_path = paste(wdpath, "figure/irttestfunctiontrimmed.png", sep = "")
png(height=600, width=600, file=file_path, type = "cairo")
plot(IRT_modeltrimmed, type = "infoSE", xlim = c(-3,3)) #test score function
dev.off()

file_path= paste(wdpath, "figure/irttestfunctiontrimmed.eps", sep="")
setEPS()
postscript(height=100, width=100, file=file_path)
plot(IRT_modeltrimmed, type = "infoSE", xlim = c(-3,3)) #test score function
dev.off()

# use the model to generate IRT scores
empty_rows = which(is.nan(rowMeans(CRATitemSettrimmed, na.rm = TRUE)))
CRAitemSetTemp2 <- CRATitemSettrimmed[-empty_rows,]
scored <- fscores(object = IRT_modeltrimmed, response.pattern = CRAitemSetTemp2)
CRATitemSettrimmed[-empty_rows,c("CRATtrimmed_IRT_score", "CRATtrimmed_IRT_score_SE")] <- scored
save(CRATitemSettrimmed, file = paste(wdpath, "data/study1/CRATitemSettrimmed.rdata", sep = ""))
IRT_model_CRAT <- IRT_modeltrimmed
save(IRT_model_CRAT, CRATitemNumbers, file = paste(wdpath, "data/study1/IRTmodelCRATitems.rdata", sep = ""))

marginalReliabilitytrimmed <- marginal_rxx(IRT_modeltrimmed)

# make table of item content and parameters
CRAitemContenttrimmedv2 <- CRAitemContent[rownames(CRATparameterstrimmed),c("prompt1", "prompt2", "prompt3", "stem")]
colnames(CRAitemContenttrimmedv2) <- c("Stimuli 1", "Stimuli 2", "Stimuli 3", "Solution")
CRATparameterstrimmedv2 <- cbind(CRAitemContenttrimmedv2, CRATparameterstrimmed)

@
\end{scriptsize}

\begin{scriptsize}
<<Study1.19, eval=TRUE, echo=FALSE, results='asis', tidy=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>=
comment <- list(pos = list(0), command = NULL)
comment$pos[[1]] <- c(nrow(CRATparameterstrimmedv2))
comment$command <- c(paste("\\hline\n",
                          "\\multicolumn{10}{l}{Notes: The values for mean, se (standard error of the mean), and n are based on individual}\\\\\n",
                          "\\multicolumn{10}{l}{administrations; the discrimination and difficulty values are based on a 2PL item response model.}\\\\\n", sep = ""))

CRATparameterstrimmedTable <- xtable(CRATparameterstrimmedv2, digits=0, caption='Item parameters for the retained CRAT items', label='tab:parameterstrimmed')
align(CRATparameterstrimmedTable) <- "lccccccccc"
digits(CRATparameterstrimmedTable) <- c(0,0,0,0,0,2,2,2,3,0)
print(CRATparameterstrimmedTable, add.to.row = comment, hline.after = c(-1,0,NULL), size='scriptsize', floating = TRUE)
@
\end{scriptsize}

Like the new CRA item set, internal consistency among the retained CRAT items was strong ($\alpha$ = \Sexpr{omega.alphatrimmed}; $\omega_{t}$ = \Sexpr{omega.ttrimmed}). Omega general ($\omega_{h}$ = \Sexpr{omega.htrimmed}) also suggests strong unidimensionality in the CRAT set, as shown in Figure \ref{fig:singlefactorcorstrimmed}. Correlations among the items had a mean value of \Sexpr{round(avg_cor_valuetrimmed,3)} and a mean 95\% confidence interval range of \Sexpr{round(meanCItrimmed,3)}. Of the 276 pairwise correlations, \Sexpr{overlappingZerotrimmed} had confidence intervals overlapping with zero; \Sexpr{sigNegtrimmed} of the remaining \Sexpr{276-overlappingZerotrimmed} had point estimates below 0. These estimates are based on pairwise administration counts among the CRAT items of \Sexpr{prettyNum(round(pwiseAdminsCRATitemsDescribe$mean, 2), big.mark=",", scientific=F)} on average (\textit{sd} = \Sexpr{prettyNum(round(pwiseAdminsCRATitemsDescribe$sd, 2), big.mark=",", scientific=F)}; \textit{Mdn} = \Sexpr{prettyNum(round(pwiseAdminsCRATitemsDescribe$median, 2), big.mark=",", scientific=F)}; minimum = \Sexpr{prettyNum(round(pwiseAdminsCRATitemsDescribe$min, 2), big.mark=",", scientific=F)}).

\begin{figure}
\ffigbox{\includegraphics[width=.75\textwidth]{figure/ICCstrimmed.png}}{\caption{Item probability functions for the retained CRAT items individually.}\label{fig:ICCstrimmed}}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}
\begin{floatrow}
\ffigbox{\includegraphics[width=.45\textwidth]{figure/singlefactorcorstrimmed.png}}{\caption{Correlations among the retained CRAT items sorted by general factor loadings.}\label{fig:singlefactorcorstrimmed}}
\ffigbox{\includegraphics[width=.45\textwidth]{figure/ICCscombinedtrimmed.png}}{\caption{Item probability functions for the retained CRAT items as a bank.}\label{fig:ICCscombinedtrimmed}}
\end{floatrow}
\end{figure}

Figures \ref{fig:ICCscombinedtrimmed} and \ref{fig:ICCstrimmed} demonstrate that most of the retained CRAT items are adequately discriminant (\textit{m} = \Sexpr{round(mean(CRATparameterstrimmed[,"discrimination"]),2)}; \textit{Mdn} = \Sexpr{round(median(CRATparameterstrimmed[,"discrimination"]),2)}). The difficulties (\textit{m} = \Sexpr{round(mean(CRATparameterstrimmed[,"difficulty"]),2)}; \textit{Mdn} = \Sexpr{round(median(CRATparameterstrimmed[,"difficulty"]),2)}) are roughly equivalent to those of the new 66 item set. Figure \ref{fig:irttestfunctiontrimmed} shows the test information and standard error functions; the marginal reliability is \Sexpr{round(marginalReliabilitytrimmed,2)}. Table \ref{tab:parameterstrimmed} includes the coefficients for all item difficulty and discrimination parameters and other descriptive statistics.

These item parameters can be used to IRT score results for these items (when the multiple choice response framework is used) in subsequent studies for the current work.

\begin{figure}
\ffigbox{\includegraphics[width=.55\textwidth]{figure/irttestfunctiontrimmed.png}}{\caption{Test information (blue) and standard error (red) functions for the retained CRAT items}\label{fig:irttestfunctiontrimmed}}
\end{figure}

\clearpage

\subsection{Validation of the final set of new CRA items}

In this subsection, we conduct a series of analyses aimed at evaluating the validity of the new CRA item set. Note that the analyses in this sub-section require access to `version 2' of the data, as the validation data relating to all other variables are not available publicly at the time of submission for peer review. The full dataset will be released at a later date, consistent with prior practice for the \textbf{[blinded for review]} project, and all reasonable requests for access prior to this time will be met (contact the senior author to request access).

The first chunk of code for these analyses (not shown in the PDF; see the Rnw version) subsets the main data frame into a more manageable object, one that contains all of the relevant scales scores and demographic variables. The scale scores include IRT-based scores for the 27 traits of the SAPA Personality Inventory \citep{Condon:2018tf, Condon:14Diss} and 6 previously validated scales in the International Cognitive Ability Resource \citep{Blum:2016, Condon:14, Guhne:2021}, as well as scale scores for the Big Five (SPI) using classical test theory (e.g., sum scores). IRT-based scoring procedures were also used for the new CRA item set (65 items) and the CRAT item set (23 items). Descriptive statistics for all of the scales are shown in Table \ref{tab:scaleDescriptives}.

\begin{scriptsize}
<<Study1.20, eval=TRUE, echo=FALSE, results='hide', fig.show='hide', tidy=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>= 
rm(list=ls()[!(ls() %in% c('wdpath', 'findcolnumber', 'score_ICAR_IRT', 'CRAitemNumbersStudy1'))])

# In this chunk of code, we're getting a matrix of scale scores; all are IRT-based except the SPI Big 5
# Here, we append CRA IRT scores onto CRAdata from CRAitemSetFinal
load(paste(wdpath, "data/study1/CRAdataStudy1version2.rdata", sep = ""))
CRAdata <- CRAdata[CRAdata$servertime < as.POSIXct("2022-06-26 12:00:00", tz="UTC"),]
dim(CRAdata)

load(paste(wdpath, "data/study1/CRAitemSetFinal.rdata", sep = ""))
load(paste(wdpath, "data/study1/CRATitemSettrimmed.rdata", sep = ""))

CRAdata <- data.frame(CRAdata, CRAitemSetFinal$CRA_new_IRT_score, CRAitemSetFinal$CRA_new_IRT_score_SE, CRATitemSettrimmed$CRATtrimmed_IRT_score, CRATitemSettrimmed$CRATtrimmed_IRT_score_SE)
names(CRAdata)[names(CRAdata) == 'CRAitemSetFinal.CRA_new_IRT_score'] <- 'CRA_new_IRT_score'
names(CRAdata)[names(CRAdata) == 'CRAitemSetFinal.CRA_new_IRT_score_SE'] <- 'CRA_new_IRT_score_SE'
names(CRAdata)[names(CRAdata) == 'CRATitemSettrimmed.CRATtrimmed_IRT_score'] <- 'CRAT_IRT_score'
names(CRAdata)[names(CRAdata) == 'CRATitemSettrimmed.CRATtrimmed_IRT_score_SE'] <- 'CRATtrimmed_IRT_score_SE'

# pull out the big five keys
makeSPI5keys <- keys.list[c("SPI_135_27_5_Agree", "SPI_135_27_5_Consc", "SPI_135_27_5_Neuro",
                            "SPI_135_27_5_Extra", "SPI_135_27_5_Open")]

SPI5key <- make.keys(itemsSPI, makeSPI5keys)
SPI5scored <- scoreItems(SPI5key, CRAdata[,itemsSPI], totals=FALSE, impute="none", min=1, max=6)
SPI5reliabilities <- SPI5scored$alpha

CRAdata <- data.frame(CRAdata, SPI5scored$scores)

# These are the items/scales we want for validity analyses
demovars <- c("RID", "age", "sex", "major", "occupation")
SPI5scores <- colnames(CRAdata)[findcolnumber(CRAdata, "SPI_135_27_5_Agree"):findcolnumber(CRAdata, "SPI_135_27_5_Open")]
SPI27irtscores <- colnames(CRAdata)[findcolnumber(CRAdata, "SPI_135_27_5_Impulsivity_score"):findcolnumber(CRAdata, "SPI_135_27_5_EasyGoingness_score")]
ICARirtscores <- colnames(CRAdata)[findcolnumber(CRAdata, "ICAR_LN_IRT_score"):findcolnumber(CRAdata, "ICAR_PR_IRT_score")]
CRAirtscores <- colnames(CRAdata)[c(findcolnumber(CRAdata, "CRA_new_IRT_score"),findcolnumber(CRAdata, "CRAT_IRT_score"))]
CRAvalidityVars <- c(demovars, SPI5scores, SPI27irtscores, ICARirtscores, CRAirtscores)

CRAvalidityData <- subset(CRAdata, select = c(CRAvalidityVars))
names(CRAvalidityData)[names(CRAvalidityData) == "RID"] <- "RID" 
names(CRAvalidityData)[names(CRAvalidityData) == "age"] <- "age" 
names(CRAvalidityData)[names(CRAvalidityData) == "sex"] <- "sex" 
