Evaluations of scale reliability vary depending on how the scales are scored. For the SPI Big Five, the standardized Cronbach's $\alpha$ values are \Sexpr{round(SPI5reliabilities[1],2)} for Agreeableness, \Sexpr{round(SPI5reliabilities[2],2)} for Conscientiousness, \Sexpr{round(SPI5reliabilities[3],2)} for Neuroticism, \Sexpr{round(SPI5reliabilities[4],2)} for Extraversion, and \Sexpr{round(SPI5reliabilities[5],2)} for Openness; these are based on the covariances of scores rather than the observed administrations, as is appropriate given the planned missingness design. For the IRT-scored scales, plots of the test information functions and standard errors are shown in Figures \ref{fig:spitif1} to \ref{fig:icartif}.

