\subsection{A mini-tutorial based on the IRT parameters for the full CRA item bank}

In the final sub-section, we use the IRT parameters identified in the prior section to re-visit the data sets in Studies 2 and 3. This is done for two reasons: (1) to show how the parameters can be used to produce generalizable scale scores that allow for the comparison of scores across samples, and (2) to compare the samples used in the current work. Recall that the data in Study 1 are highly overlapping with the data used to derive the IRT parameters; Studies 2 and 3 are `out-of-sample' in this case.

The procedures are relatively straight-forward. We need (a) the scoring function, (b) the scoring parameters, and (c) the data to be scored. We have been using the IRT scoring function throughout this project --- it was actually included in the very first code chunk, visible in the .Rnw version of the file. For transparency, we print the code of that function here for reference; it is essentially a wrapper around the \texttt{fscores()} function in the mirt package \citep{Chalmers:2012ut}.

\begin{scriptsize}
<<Study4.9, eval=TRUE, echo=TRUE, results='markup', fig.show='hide', tidy=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>= 
rm(list=ls()[!(ls() %in% c('findcolnumber', 'keys.list', 'wdpath', 'score_ICAR_IRT'))])

score_ICAR_IRT
@
\end{scriptsize}

The scoring parameters were identified in the previous subsection. As previously stated, the model can be downloaded directly from the OSF page (\href{tinyurl.com/2a8vx6n8}{tinyurl.com/2a8vx6n8}); look for the file named `IRTmodelCRAitemsFullBank.rdata'. In the next code chunk, we show it being loaded (after being downloaded). 

\begin{scriptsize}
<<Study4.10, eval=TRUE, echo=TRUE, results='asis', fig.show='hide', tidy=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>= 
load(paste(wdpath, "data/study4/IRTmodelCRAitemsFullBank.rdata", sep = ""))
@
\end{scriptsize}

Then we load the data for the studies and trim the data frames to focus on the CRA items only. These data have previously be transformed to 1s and 0s to reflect correct or incorrect responses, respectively.

\begin{scriptsize}
<<Study4.11, eval=TRUE, echo=TRUE, results='markup', fig.show='hide', tidy=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>= 
# Study 2
load(paste(wdpath, "data/study2/CRAdataStudy2.rdata", sep = ""))
CRAdataStudy2 <- subset(study2dataCRAonly, select = c(CRAitemNumbersStudy2))
dim(CRAdataStudy2)
# Recall that 63 items were left at the end of Study 2

# Study 3
load(paste(wdpath, "data/study3/CRAdataStudy3.rdata", sep = ""))
CRAdataStudy3 <- subset(study3dataCRAonly, select = c(CRAitemNumbersStudy3))
dim(CRAdataStudy3)
# Recall that 66 items were left at the end of Study 3

@
\end{scriptsize}

Now, use the IRT scoring function and IRT parameters to score the data for each study and then plot to compare the scores across samples.

\begin{scriptsize}
<<Study4.12, eval=TRUE, echo=TRUE, results='asis', fig.show='hide', tidy=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>= 
# Study 2
CRAstudy2scores = score_ICAR_IRT(dataset = CRAdataStudy2, IRT_model = IRT_model_ICAR_CRA, t_score = FALSE)

# Study 3
CRAstudy3scores = score_ICAR_IRT(dataset = CRAdataStudy3, IRT_model = IRT_model_ICAR_CRA, t_score = FALSE)
@
\end{scriptsize}

\begin{scriptsize}
<<Study4.13, eval=TRUE, echo=TRUE, results='markup', tidy=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE>>=
file_path = paste(wdpath, "figure/comparesamples.png", sep = "")
png(height=600, width=1200, file=file_path, type = "cairo")
plot1 <- ggplot(CRAstudy2scores, aes(x=ICAR_CRA)) + 
  geom_histogram(bins = 100) + labs(x="CRA IRT-based scores") + 
  lims(x = c(-2,2), y = c(0,25)) 
plot2 <- ggplot(CRAstudy3scores, aes(x=ICAR_CRA)) + 
  geom_histogram(bins = 100) + labs(x="CRA IRT-based scores") + 
  lims(x = c(-2,2), y = c(0,25)) 
grid.arrange(plot1, plot2, ncol=2)
dev.off()
@
\end{scriptsize}

\begin{figure}
\ffigbox{\includegraphics[width=.85\textwidth]{figure/comparesamples.png}}{\caption{Distribution of CRA scores in Study 2 (left) and Study 3 (right) based on IRT scores for the full item bank}\label{fig:distributions}}
\end{figure}

Figure \ref{fig:distributions} shows the distributions of IRT scores for the CRA scales in Studies 2 and 3. The undergraduate sample from Study 2 (the distribution on the left) has slight evidence of a floor effect but the scores are mainly distributed across the range of difficulty. The online sample from Study 3 has a restricted range on the high end, but a similarly broad range of scores overall. Though it is not possible to know what factors contribute to low scores in either sample, it seems very likely that low scores are influenced, at least in part, by low engagement with the CRA items. In sum, it appears that the IRT parameters of the CRA item bank provide a substantially wide range of scores in samples like those that are typically used in psychology research, without much restriction of range in either direction. In combination with the other aspects of reliability and validity evaluated in the prior studies, this provides strong evidence of the utility of the CRA item bank for the assessment of associative ability.

\center{\textbf{FIN}}

